In article <RATINOX.93May24144403@denali.ccs.neu.edu>, ratinox@denali.ccs.neu.edu (Richard Pieri) writes:
|> If you're using assembly, you must want speed and/or very small code. If
|> this is the case, then why on earth are you even bothering with a compiled
|> language? If it's not, then why on earth are you even bothering with
|> assembly?

IMHO Conrad's approach is quite reasonable.  He wanted to know if it was
easy to incorporate small sections of assembly into a game written in C++,
and whether the approach was worthwhile.

The answer, of course, depends entirely on the type of game, and the
computing power of the target machine.  A strategy game would be extremely
well suited to a high-level language, for example.  Certain graphic effects
would be well suited to tightly coded C or assembly language.

Conrad, my only recommendation is that you first determine the requirements of
your program.  You can make a back of the envelop calculation of the amount of
video data that needs to be moved to the screen each frame, and can then judge
the number of cycles you will need.  You should be able to then write some
simple benchmark tests to see if your compiler can generate that throughput.
It beats writing a whole lot of code and having to throw it out.

I can't help with the specifics of ASM inclusion, since I haven't used the
Turbo C++/ASM tools.  I have done it on the Mac and Atari ST with several
compilers and it was relatively straightforward, though.

